#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133610 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/id #uuid "cb31a55d-6107-509a-80e2-9bdffcaff6e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5590" :question/problem "![](images/98/98b9eaa366bb0c1dcf899eb231b34e9704335a294d4b69c6af10197eb14929ce.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{2}{3}+3x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/b3/b36e5a28c59c1ab0be811fb94fec4dc018b6687229f4acb3ef11a745fdeb6631.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133611 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/id #uuid "c5097abf-c541-5c52-85d3-1e56a4e53cb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5591" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A \\mathbf x = \\mathbf b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where \n\n![](images/88/8803a165894746f62c8d27530eebd791d03cfc16ee8da0dd3e176c30a400ee4a.png)\n\nThe least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 144\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ -36 & 144 & -12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac{1}{3}+ 4x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/1f/1f35d807d723ae25214ce1cdd9fdb42c5111f6a0495a5f453e8a04b415863466.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133634 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/id #uuid "8a39f109-9436-55f8-bbb8-87253668378d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5963" :question/problem "![](images/86/86e05af9d6062530450e56f5e292106413301a869e765f413dcbe6865a6ce8a5.png)\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a-b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as \n![](images/c2/c2daef6c8fee0dd47562395ec76a2912eaea98e3a873d6319553ed281745d210.png)\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & -2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}80 & -60 & -20 \\\\ -60 & 45 & 15 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 5\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}32 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-b+c=(-20)-(-24)+(-20) = -16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61f6d487-6ec9-51e0-b88b-fc9df19cbc3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367431} {:answer/id #uuid "03cb6b05-17f1-5921-96f3-cc50808a26ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367432} {:answer/id #uuid "9d682f1d-3ed3-5788-8567-22a0ee51538d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367433} {:answer/id #uuid "7825acfd-a465-5c06-8fd7-d1224330d940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367434} {:answer/id #uuid "3e78bee4-76bc-5377-abfd-3838c1bbc207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7825acfd-a465-5c06-8fd7-d1224330d940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367434} :answer-field/id #uuid "a655004e-1ad8-59c2-9d78-74eb309d05be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fcaaa14-b976-59cf-b555-9129a6aa2fb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-136821" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ above. If the normal equation corresponding to the system $Ax=b$ is given below, what is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ -17 & 17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}17 & -17 \\\\ -17 & 17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=17+(-17)+15=15.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "056bde88-c84a-542b-b854-757f76eb5537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266192} {:answer/id #uuid "11577138-092f-5405-8add-1bf21e092d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266289} {:answer/id #uuid "1af21ba1-ec9f-5759-8519-d9a10d13d566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266374} {:answer/id #uuid "603109b1-cdf0-531f-bf87-a3d70e3bcf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266993} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5b4264-6657-5997-9a3f-3e00aff7f007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "603109b1-cdf0-531f-bf87-a3d70e3bcf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266993} :answer-field/id #uuid "126b6f84-805d-591b-a564-13937bad6be3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69f4362f-6b4d-5c30-8b5c-dedd940a5e83" :question/math-academy-id "q-136822" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}40 & -20 & -60 \\\\ -20 & 10 & a \\\\ c & 30 & 90\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ b \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}40 & 20 & -60 \\\\ -20 & 10 & 30 \\\\ -60 & 30 & 90\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=30+5+(-60)=-25.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32b3d1a3-e2c5-53af-933b-517ec9a0d72b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266589} {:answer/id #uuid "5c45d0cb-d619-548e-8823-23b2000f9c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266959} {:answer/id #uuid "5f359553-9efb-5c8e-94ff-571d277b5bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266983} {:answer/id #uuid "d1706cd6-61bf-52a6-816c-b55ddc048d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268018} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d0f2d7-43a5-560a-91a8-6d0fe37c6f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f359553-9efb-5c8e-94ff-571d277b5bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266983} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ca2f21a-e889-5f63-95ad-6473117737ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9599aec-b36e-5461-9425-547d03dffdb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-136875" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=-3\\end{aligned}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}20 & a & 10 \\\\ c & 80 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -20 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ b \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a-b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}20 & -40 & 10 \\\\ -40 & 80 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -20 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ -44 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\na-b+c=(-40)-(-44)+(-40)=-36.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "612c1237-1d75-5779-901e-394b60ae8abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415123} {:answer/id #uuid "985ff754-4000-553d-84fd-3adae2a90775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415127} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f160c1-cc03-5355-b516-d2d19b494551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415129} {:answer/id #uuid "b6774fe7-0b94-51e6-8366-d780b5d12442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415130} {:answer/id #uuid "fe32f448-8a3a-522b-9c2c-5ad76f739b65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "985ff754-4000-553d-84fd-3adae2a90775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415127} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf2aa749-d040-57c2-b657-f15f0f4f8d98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "090045f3-969e-5b87-b1e6-6f48922501d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-136945" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+3b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 4 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -6 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -6 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{3}=-2,\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+(-2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+3b+c=0+3\\cdot (-2)+1=-5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a5a9c23-8d4c-5a61-af41-8b6d2c96bcb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266659} {:answer/id #uuid "b2fa2a26-e812-549e-8835-6295aa5319d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267747} {:answer/id #uuid "cf723ad7-5492-59ea-aec1-79f455aab318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267999} {:answer/id #uuid "db4cc96a-2085-57a1-9317-5c474924f1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268084} {:answer/id #uuid "f40e0d44-f7ca-5e19-a8b9-8693564691ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf723ad7-5492-59ea-aec1-79f455aab318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267999} :answer-field/id #uuid "d11dceef-fa44-5f21-b07d-72b4a644c05f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef71613a-c794-54f5-9a74-68efbe48ffea" :question/math-academy-id "q-137034" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,+\\,{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+2b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\n\nFrom the second row, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{5}-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the first row, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+2b+c=1+2\\cdot 0+(-1)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a1c90f3-c1b6-573f-ab6c-07ec11bf9f28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266365} {:answer/id #uuid "4aab64f6-7242-5884-9cd4-b0a3a4a9143e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266805} {:answer/id #uuid "5bba4d55-7c36-5233-8c45-c58d4705d39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266956} {:answer/id #uuid "90976ab1-62e5-54ff-a6c5-42bb9d3b1bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267431} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4dfd78-93dd-5e18-9a5e-04b304c3c8d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a1c90f3-c1b6-573f-ab6c-07ec11bf9f28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266365} :answer-field/id #uuid "5364be24-88fb-5a2f-b7c6-4e394496b06e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26d67fbf-4de7-566c-9152-9d1736d17888" :question/math-academy-id "q-137061" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}30 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 120\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{2}{1}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa14c10-e738-56f6-ba1c-e071a121f17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415109} {:answer/id #uuid "3394fe0c-1ef5-5d2a-acd6-6a75d0644ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415118} {:answer/id #uuid "796589ac-95c1-56f9-b1db-31d0e03ed1d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415125} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ae1013-f366-5e65-90ff-b5b5ec91675a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415128} {:answer/id #uuid "f6952c2b-bc0a-5679-9e51-c3da4a58655d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0ae1013-f366-5e65-90ff-b5b5ec91675a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415128} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed476833-2f74-5fd6-89de-22e15124bc5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01d4a87d-4b65-5a4c-9736-928bedaa6703" :question/math-academy-id "q-137086" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $12a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -10 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 30\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{3}+3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n12a+\\frac{b}{c}=12\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{3})+\\frac{3}{1}=-17.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0366dc6-1996-570e-840e-8c80856c8afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367452} {:answer/id #uuid "2b06eb22-5e54-52d9-a5d8-de25d113e619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367453} {:answer/id #uuid "425e4639-bd5f-5d47-b6e2-c94ebaa608bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367454} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a6a34f-befd-59cd-bdc7-6ba87f58be40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367455} {:answer/id #uuid "977b53fc-6878-5223-bf26-85404a2bfdba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b06eb22-5e54-52d9-a5d8-de25d113e619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367453} :answer-field/id #uuid "c701115a-b609-5cd4-a698-790dbbe0d2a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f080e633-9838-5e96-8287-b7abe35cec6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-137091" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $5a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 24\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 12 & 6 \\\\ 12 & 24 & 12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 12 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n5a+\\frac{b}{c}=5\\cdot 1+\\frac{(-2)}{1}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e85101-f9b2-5d74-83c6-aabdd8fc2745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415114} {:answer/id #uuid "2baff1db-5ead-50b2-af5a-abe277ae4820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415115} {:answer/id #uuid "2ccd28ea-578f-5414-8332-a6408e6df178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415116} {:answer/id #uuid "2e8878bb-5944-584d-9b48-2add56ecc5fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415117} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd78f8e-e662-5c98-aa4d-0c408cd252ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cd78f8e-e662-5c98-aa4d-0c408cd252ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415126} :answer-field/id #uuid "a27c023d-32d0-5cf3-ade4-1f1674089f41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a92698e-17e4-57b8-8191-7aa9adc0e63f" :question/math-academy-id "q-137092" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $6a+\\frac{4b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & 4 \\\\ -12 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n6a+\\frac{4b}{c}=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}{1}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b66e8f9-798c-5fb5-9702-3848b450ff05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266376} {:answer/id #uuid "1c88a6e9-e50e-5742-b36a-eb35a929b299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266387} {:answer/id #uuid "275bb128-5a9f-5d1f-abdd-0647ca8896e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266494} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed0a256-a969-57ba-a38b-d639f21e2e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266980} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3b665d-ddd7-5f8f-a302-3a3d75a9b19e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ed0a256-a969-57ba-a38b-d639f21e2e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266980} :answer-field/id #uuid "343b4b87-3893-598e-8c73-4fe5b3ba88f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93179ecb-11e4-5253-ab06-7702070386da" :question/math-academy-id "q-137097" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-9a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 \\\\ -27 & 27\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 & -9 \\\\ -27 & 27 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-9a+\\frac{b}{c}=-9\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})+\\frac{1}{1}=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027310f9-ee0d-507a-bc28-d9da862bc3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323994} {:answer/id #uuid "13cbf72a-775b-5500-b4ce-fd7dee8abc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324150} {:answer/id #uuid "6058f5f8-765c-528f-b9e3-77d370e52e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324776} {:answer/id #uuid "df18dbbf-d1af-5d62-9ba8-07e3f6047abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325920} {:answer/id #uuid "df1f5f2f-9dda-564b-8601-3ba072795d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "027310f9-ee0d-507a-bc28-d9da862bc3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323994} :answer-field/id #uuid "58847018-c5c9-5b9a-9650-5b07e75b721b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d34cdae0-7b23-59fc-a8bc-f0d99444106f" :question/math-academy-id "q-40921" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $3a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -18 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n3a+\\frac{b}{c}=3\\cdot \\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{1}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33ec9bd3-99d5-5104-8812-7b53252709d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324385} {:answer/id #uuid "444cc429-dfb0-5a5b-b8d4-d4fc2403cd81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324522} {:answer/id #uuid "4c018f0f-7adf-5ead-8130-41b36d9554f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324593} {:answer/id #uuid "67fb75dd-92e4-5c22-83db-40e298fd59cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324838} {:answer/id #uuid "e675d251-277e-5b93-99a7-e7f84b39dcf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67fb75dd-92e4-5c22-83db-40e298fd59cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324838} :answer-field/id #uuid "5027b317-01e3-5fb7-adb1-b9069ee0de3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da9cd726-dc83-58c0-961c-2d8f4017268b" :question/math-academy-id "q-40941" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $6a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}15 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 60\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}15 & 30 & 10 \\\\ 30 & 60 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}15 & 30 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n6a+\\frac{b}{c}=6\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{(-2)}{1}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1299ade0-03c4-5049-abac-d1b905ea647d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368556} {:answer/id #uuid "de3e0276-368e-58fc-81ca-2ba8066692a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368557} {:answer/id #uuid "ce89137e-5a01-54bb-9780-320b4e37ec64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368558} {:answer/id #uuid "ce900d5f-43f0-5f77-adbc-98050b478ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368559} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8250d4-f373-5760-9fc7-2ed30b0938ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de3e0276-368e-58fc-81ca-2ba8066692a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368557} :answer-field/id #uuid "1812231c-b731-57f0-8e96-cc3da0e4fe33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8912c2e3-5605-5590-8ee8-b25e93901622" :question/math-academy-id "q-42245" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+4c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\n\n From the second row, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(-3)+2{x}_{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-4-2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -3 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+4c=(-2)+(-3)+4\\cdot 0=-5.\n$$"}]}
